WEBVTT

0
00:00:03.769 --> 00:00:06.039
When managing traditional networks,

1
00:00:06.170 --> 00:00:09.529
we obsess over bandwidth and average latency.

2
00:00:10.250 --> 00:00:12.289
If the average speed is fast,

3
00:00:12.630 --> 00:00:14.949
we assume the system is healthy.

4
00:00:15.369 --> 00:00:17.129
But in distributed AI,

5
00:00:17.370 --> 00:00:19.530
the average is often not enough.

6
00:00:19.770 --> 00:00:21.930
What matters is predictability.

7
00:00:22.920 --> 00:00:24.920
One of the biggest hidden challenges

8
00:00:24.920 --> 00:00:28.579
in AI infrastructure isn't consistent latency,

9
00:00:28.840 --> 00:00:30.819
it's micro variability.

10
00:00:31.360 --> 00:00:32.709
We call this jitter.

11
00:00:33.950 --> 00:00:35.229
At AI scale,

12
00:00:35.680 --> 00:00:38.180
small variability compounds quickly.

13
00:00:39.400 --> 00:00:44.540
Jitter refers to small variations in network timing and delivery behavior.

14
00:00:45.349 --> 00:00:48.619
It means your network packet delivery times are fluctuating.

15
00:00:49.990 --> 00:00:51.830
In a standard cloud app,

16
00:00:51.990 --> 00:00:55.119
a 5 millisecond fluctuation is invisible,

17
00:00:55.430 --> 00:00:58.299
but in a synchronous AI training cluster,

18
00:00:58.509 --> 00:01:01.529
a 5 millisecond delay on a single packet

19
00:01:01.790 --> 00:01:04.269
can ripple across coordinated workloads.

20
00:01:05.129 --> 00:01:05.138
Because

21
00:01:05.569 --> 00:01:07.639
AI nodes are tightly coupled,

22
00:01:07.889 --> 00:01:13.739
they can only proceed as fast as the slowest participating node in any given cycle.

23
00:01:14.129 --> 00:01:18.269
If GPU number 4 experiences a tiny network jitter,

24
00:01:18.690 --> 00:01:20.209
GPUs 1, 2,

25
00:01:20.290 --> 00:01:21.110
and 3

26
00:01:21.330 --> 00:01:24.669
may spend time waiting for coordination to catch up.

27
00:01:25.250 --> 00:01:28.370
Progress across the workload slows down.

28
00:01:29.449 --> 00:01:29.949
Now,

29
00:01:30.089 --> 00:01:31.099
scale that up.

30
00:01:31.360 --> 00:01:34.650
2 milliseconds of idle time doesn't sound like much,

31
00:01:34.970 --> 00:01:40.629
but when you have 10,000 GPUs running thousands of coordination cycles every hour,

32
00:01:41.050 --> 00:01:45.129
that jitter creates compounding coordination overhead

33
00:01:45.580 --> 00:01:47.330
across your entire cluster.

34
00:01:48.319 --> 00:01:48.910
Suddenly,

35
00:01:49.160 --> 00:01:51.059
you aren't just losing milliseconds.

36
00:01:51.900 --> 00:01:56.160
You are accumulating significant compute inefficiency over time

37
00:01:56.559 --> 00:02:01.760
and consuming substantial infrastructure resources with reduced efficiency.

38
00:02:03.239 --> 00:02:04.769
In distributed AI

39
00:02:05.250 --> 00:02:08.910
performance instability becomes a system level problem.

40
00:02:10.149 --> 00:02:11.949
You must architect your networks

41
00:02:11.949 --> 00:02:15.089
to prioritize predictable coordination behavior

42
00:02:15.270 --> 00:02:16.169
over peak

43
00:02:16.350 --> 00:02:17.610
theoretical speed.